A customer says her child became ill after a visit.

The message also names a staff member and mentions a regulator.

Most automation demos treat this as a writing task. Read the complaint, produce a polite reply, move on.

I built my Review & Reply Agent to do the opposite.

It stopped.

## Support work taught me to respect the hand-off

I spent five years in a BPO moving through technical support, live queue management, training and sales support.

The job changed, but one rule stayed consistent: some problems belong with the person holding the right authority.

A support agent should not invent a policy. A trainer should not hide a weak process behind a better script. A live queue should not look healthy because the difficult cases were counted in the wrong place.

Years later, the same rule shaped this build.

The goal was never to make an AI sound calm in public. The goal was to reduce the reading and sorting work without letting a model speak for the owner during a serious complaint.

## The first useful feature was a refusal

The workflow reads each review and checks for clear warning signs.

A rating of three or below goes to the owner. So do reports involving illness, injury, hygiene, discrimination, staff conduct, legal action, chargebacks, regulators or press.

If the model returns an answer the workflow cannot read, the review goes to the owner. No guess fills the gap.

Routine praise follows a different path. The system writes a draft and puts it in an approval queue.

Nothing posts on its own.

## Three runs tested the boundary

I ran three live cases from start to finish.

A five-star review praised a specific dish and the staff. The workflow wrote a relevant reply and queued it for approval.

A two-star review alleged food poisoning, named a staff member and threatened legal action. The workflow escalated it.

The complaint involving a child’s illness also escalated. The model left the reply blank.

At first, the empty field looked broken. I changed the alert to say why no draft was written: a suggested response risked doing more harm than good.

The three runs averaged 3.48 seconds from intake to delivered alert.

## A green workflow was not enough

I later installed the repository from scratch, the way a stranger would.

The test used a clean n8n instance, real credentials, a real test message and a real Telegram delivery. The pass condition was not a row of green boxes. It was a returned Telegram message ID.

The test exposed the difference between a workflow running on its creator’s machine and one another person is able to install.

It also reinforced an older lesson from support work: completion is not the same as resolution.

## The part worth selling is the boundary

The agent handles the repetitive work: reading, sorting, checking rules and preparing safe drafts.

The owner keeps the irreversible decision.

I did not build it to remove a person from customer care. I built it to make sure the person arrives at the right moment, with the full review already organised.

The safest review agent is fast because it knows where its job ends.

Built by handlit.
